17.2 A paid upgrade applies to the flights shown and is not refundable
once the first upgraded flight has been flown.
17.3 Upgrades paid for with miles or points are not available on this
fare.

18. Payment
18.1 The price of the ticket is charged in full at the time of booking.
18.2 The card used to pay may need to be shown at check-in.
18.3 A payment in another currency is converted by the card issuer,
which may add a fee.

19. Check-in and boarding
19.1 Check-in closes 47 minutes before departure on flights within the
country.
19.2 Boarding begins about 38 minutes before departure, and the aircraft
door closes 13 minutes before departure.
19.3 A traveler who has not checked in by the cut-off time may lose the
seat.

20. Conduct
20.1 The airline may refuse to carry a traveler whose conduct puts the
safety of the flight at risk.
20.2 A traveler refused for conduct is not owed a refund.

21. Help at the airport
21.1 Wheelchair help is free of charge and should be asked for at least
48 hours before departure.
21.2 Trained service animals travel free of charge in the cabin.

22. Taxes and fees
22.1 Government taxes and fees are set by the authorities and may change
before travel.
22.2 A new or higher tax that comes into force after purchase may be
collected at the airport.

23. Travel documents
23.1 Each traveler is responsible for carrying the identity and travel
documents that every place on the trip requires.
23.2 The airline may refuse to carry a traveler whose documents are
missing, damaged or out of date, and no refund is owed in that case.
23.3 A fine charged to the airline because a traveler lacked the right
documents may be passed on to that traveler.

24. Liability for bags
24.1 The airline's liability for a lost, damaged or delayed bag is
limited by the contract of carriage and by law.
24.2 Cash, keys, medicine, jewelry, computers and other valuables should
be carried in the cabin and never packed in a checked bag.
24.3 Normal wear to a bag, such as scuffs, small cuts and dents, is not
damage the airline pays for.
24.4 Damage to a bag must be reported within seven days of arrival.

25. Lost and delayed bags
25.1 A bag that does not arrive should be reported at the airport before
the traveler leaves the baggage hall.
25.2 Reasonable costs for essential items while a bag is delayed may be
paid back when receipts are sent in.
25.3 A bag that has not been found within 21 days is treated as lost.

26. Health and fitness to fly
26.1 A traveler who needs medical oxygen or a medical device on board
must tell the airline at least 48 hours before departure.
26.2 The airline may ask for a doctor's note when a traveler's fitness
to fly is in doubt.
26.3 A traveler late in pregnancy may need a doctor's note that gives
the expected date of birth.

27. Batteries and dangerous goods
27.1 Spare batteries for phones, cameras and computers must travel in
the cabin, never in a checked bag.
27.2 Fuel, gas cylinders, fireworks and other dangerous goods may not be
carried at all.
27.3 A bag found to hold a forbidden item may be opened and the item
removed.

28. Overbooking
28.1 A flight may be sold with more tickets than seats.
28.2 When a flight has too many travelers, the airline first asks for
volunteers to give up their seats in exchange for agreed benefits.
28.3 A traveler who is denied boarding against their wishes is rebooked
and paid compensation as the law requires.

29. Connections
29.1 Connections on a single ticket are planned with enough time to
change aircraft.
29.2 A connection that a traveler makes between two separate tickets is
not protected, and a missed flight on the second ticket is the
traveler's own cost.
29.3 Bags are checked through to the final stop only when every flight
is on one ticket.

30. Flights run by partner airlines
30.1 A flight sold by this airline may be run by a partner airline.
30.2 On a flight run by a partner, that partner's rules on bags, seats
and service may apply.
30.3 Check-in for a flight run by a partner may take place at that
partner's counter.

31. Earning miles or points
31.1 Miles or points are earned on this fare at the rate shown in the
rules of the program the traveler belongs to.
31.2 The part of a ticket paid for with travel credit may earn at a
lower rate.
31.3 Miles or points for a flight cannot be added more than six months
after it was flown.

32. Price changes
32.1 The price of a ticket is fixed once it has been issued.
32.2 If the fare for the same flight falls later, the difference is not
paid back unless the ticket is changed.

33. Information shared with authorities
33.1 Details of the booking may be shared with border and security
authorities where the law requires it.
33.2 A traveler's date of birth and gender as printed on their ID may be
needed before check-in.

34. Requests and complaints
34.1 A request about this ticket may be made through
https://www.example.com/ at any time.
34.2 A complaint should be sent in writing within 45 days of travel,
with the record locator and the ticket number.
34.3 The airline answers a written complaint within 30 days.

35. Food and drink
35.1 Soft drinks and a small snack are served free of charge on most
flights.
35.2 Meals may be bought on longer flights, while supplies last.
35.3 Alcohol may be served only by the crew, and only to travelers aged
21 and over.

36. Devices on board
36.1 Phones and other devices must be set to flight mode when the crew
asks.
36.2 Wi-Fi is not included in this fare and may be bought on board where
it is offered.
36.3 Wi-Fi that was bought and did not work may be paid back as travel
credit.

37. Smoking
37.1 Smoking is not allowed on board, and that includes electronic
cigarettes.
37.2 Tampering with a smoke detector on board is against the law.

38. The contract of carriage
38.1 These rules form part of the contract of carriage between the
traveler and the airline.
38.2 Where these rules and the contract of carriage differ, the contract
of carriage applies.
38.3 The full contract of carriage may be read at
https://www.example.com/ at any time.

